## PostgreSQL vs SQLite Evaluation

| Criteria | SQLite | PostgreSQL |
|----------|--------|------------|
| **Setup** | Zero install — single file, built into Python | Requires installing PostgreSQL server, creating DB/user |
| **Performance (single user)** | ✅ Excellent for localhost demo | Overkill — adds latency from network layer |
| **Performance (multi user)** | ❌ Poor — file-level locking | ✅ Designed for concurrent connections |
| **Time-series support** | ❌ None — must manually query by timestamp | ✅ TimescaleDB extension for time-series |
| **Deployment complexity** | ✅ Nothing to deploy — `.db` file ships with project | ⚠️ Needs running PostgreSQL service or Docker |
| **Capstone impression** | Adequate for demo | Looks more "production-grade" to evaluators |
| **Data volume** | Good up to ~1GB (your SWaT.csv is ~7MB) | Unlimited — handles TB-scale |
| **SQL compatibility** | Most SQL features, no JSONB, limited ALTER TABLE | Full SQL, JSONB, window functions, CTEs |
| **Portability** | ✅ Copy one file = done | ❌ Needs DB dump/restore |

> [!TIP]
> **Recommendation**: Use **SQLite** — your dataset is small (~15K rows), single-user localhost demo, zero setup friction. You can always migrate to PostgreSQL later since SQLAlchemy abstracts the DB layer. If your advisor specifically values seeing PostgreSQL, it's a 10-minute change.

